                <pb facs="1230" n="1106" sameAs="frus:frus1923v02/pg1106"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.672/124: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Special Mission at Lausanne</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-07-21T13:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 21, 1923—1
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>252. The views expressed in Mission’s 548 of July 21, and especially the
                    statements you made to Ismet and reported in the first paragraph are fully
                    approved by the Department. You should endeavor to <pb facs="1231" n="1107"
                        xml:id="pg1107"/> meet every reasonable request of the Turks and use the
                    utmost patience in your dealings. But as you have indicated in your telegram,
                    the Department would not wish you to give consideration to a treaty which leaves
                    fundamental issues for later settlement.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
